# Phase 259: operator catalog snapshots. Somebody’s library is not this repository’s content.
# Root-anchored: a bare `import/` would also ignore any future source directory of that name.
/import/

# Playwright's own run artifacts. They carry absolute local paths, which is exactly what
# deploy/ci/acceptance/redact-artifacts.sh exists to keep out of anything shared.
deploy/ci/acceptance/test-results/
deploy/ci/acceptance/playwright-report/

# Scratch directory for the projectiond image-mount gate.
.projectiond-image-gate/

# ...and for the publisher-to-mount gate, which needs one for the same reason: bind propagation requires a
# host path on a shared mount, so the working directory has to live beside the repository rather than under
# /tmp. Both gates remove their own on the way out; this is what keeps an INTERRUPTED run from leaving
# untracked files behind, which is the only state where it matters.
.projection-publisher-gate/

# ...and for the media-server data-plane gate, for the same reason again. This one also holds generated media
# and a media server's whole config directory, so leaving it behind would be several megabytes of untracked
# noise rather than a few files.
.projection-jellyfin-gate/

# ...and for the PLEX data-plane gate, which holds all of that plus a Plex config directory and a transcode
# scratch area. The gate deletes it on success and on failure; this is the belt to that brace.
.projection-plex-gate/

# ...and for the EMBY data-plane gate. Same reasons, plus one this gate has that the other two do not: because
# Emby refuses an unauthenticated direct play, the paced consumer needs a credential, and the gate writes a
# throwaway access token to a file in here for it to read. The cleanup trap deletes the directory on success
# and on failure, and this is what stops an INTERRUPTED run leaving that file where `git add -A` could reach
# it.
.projection-emby-gate/

# ...and for the THREE-SERVER CONCURRENCY gate, which holds three media servers' whole config directories at
# once. It had no entry here; every other gate in this family does, and the reason each of those gives applies
# to it unchanged.
.projection-three-gate/

# ...and for the RCLONE/WEBDAV COMPARISON CONTROL (G22). Same reasons as the four above, plus the one the Emby
# entry names: this gate mints a throwaway endpoint credential per run and writes it to a file inside its run
# directory, because a credential on a command line is in an argv, a container inspect and a shell history.
# The cleanup trap deletes the directory on success and on failure. This is what stops an INTERRUPTED run from
# leaving that file where `git add -A` could reach it.
.projection-rclone-gate/

# ...and for the PHASE 2b MULTI-FRONTEND COMPARISON harness, which is the same reason with three times the
# exposure: it mints TWO throwaway endpoint credentials per run (the original and the one R2 rotates to),
# writes both to files inside its run directory, and stands up three arms whose mounts and client caches all
# live under that root. An interrupted run leaves all of it where `git add -A` could reach it.
.projection-multi-frontend-comparison-gate/

# ...and for the ACCESS-LEASE gate (G24–G26) and the PATH-LIFECYCLE gate (G27). Both declare a gate root the
# same way the entries above do and neither had one here; the reason every entry above gives applies to them
# unchanged.
.projection-lease-gate/
.projection-lifecycle-gate/

# ...and for the THREE gates that take an operator-supplied corpus: the offline TorBox mount gate, the
# generic real-provider gate, and the REAL TorBox gate.
#
# THE LAST OF THESE IS THE FIRST GATE IN THIS FAMILY THAT LEAVES A FILE BEHIND ON PURPOSE, so its entry is
# doing more work than the others'. `.projection-torbox-real-gate/evidence/reads-*.json` is preserved by
# design — on success as a hard precondition of deleting the run directory, and on failure best-effort from
# the EXIT trap — because it is the only record of what was read through the mount. That file carries
# operator-chosen labels and the exact object size, which is corpus detail this repository deliberately keeps
# out of git; the gate writes it at mode 0700 under a directory it chmods 0700, and this entry is what stops
# `git add -A` reaching it regardless. The other two never intend to leave anything, and this is the belt to
# that brace for an INTERRUPTED run of either.
.projection-torbox-gate/
.projection-real-provider-gate/
.projection-torbox-real-gate/

# THE PHASE 3 RELIABILITY LOOP'S ROOT, and it holds MORE than an interrupted run's scratch: its evidence
# directory is where every run's verdict log lives, deliberately, because the run directory the cleanup
# contract deletes is no place for the record of what was measured. It also holds two copies of the
# operator's secrets at 0600 for the length of a run. Neither belongs anywhere `git add -A` reaches.
.projection-reliability-loop-gate/

# The consumer-attachment check’s root. It holds no secret — it contacts no provider and mints no
# credential — but an interrupted run leaves a scratch tree, and nothing here belongs where a bare
# `git add -A` reaches.
.projection-consumer-attachment-check/

# THE ROOTS OF EVERY GATE THIS LINE OF WORK ADDED AFTER THE ONES ABOVE, AND THE OMISSION WAS INCIDENTAL
# RATHER THAN DELIBERATE.
#
# Each holds the same two things the reliability loop's root does: an interrupted run's scratch tree, and an
# `evidence/` directory that is kept ON PURPOSE — verdict logs, a failing arm's daemon log, a decoder's
# stderr — because the run directory the cleanup contract deletes is no place for the record of what was
# measured. The Phase 7 and Phase 8 gate roots also hold a 0600 copy of the operator's two secret files for
# the length of a run.
#
# NOTHING OF THE KIND WAS EVER TRACKED, and that was luck rather than structure: these directories simply
# did not exist on a machine where anybody ran `git add -A`. This makes it structural.
.projection-phase7-gate/
.projection-phase8-gate/
.projection-phase8-rehearsal/
.projection-alpha-acceptance/
.projection-restart-topology-gate/
.projection-recovery-gate/
.projection-stale-mount-gate/
.projection-serve-death-gate/
.projection-mount-truth-gate/
.projection-mount-health-gate/
.projection-sustained-outage-gate/
